@startuml
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Segoe UI
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam ArrowColor #0b7a75
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #0b7a75
skinparam DatabaseBorderColor #6b7280
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #f3f4f6
skinparam NoteBorderColor #9ca3af
skinparam StateBorderColor #0b7a75
skinparam StateBackgroundColor #ffffff

title The life of one session token: minted once, re-stamped on every hop,\nverified at the UniversalNavigator — the gate only carries it

[*] --> NoSession

state "No session" as NoSession

state "Minting — session establishment" as Mint {
  state "GUID decided" as MintGuid
  state "Token assembled and signed" as MintSign
  MintGuid --> MintSign
}

state "Live session" as Live {
  state "Held by the browser" as Held
  state "Verified at the UniversalNavigator" as Verify
  state "Re-stamped" as Restamp
  state "Persisted as a repository row" as Persist

  Held --> Verify : the Gate collects session_token\nand passes it to the UniversalNavigator
  Verify --> Restamp : signature verifies
  Restamp --> Persist : the UniversalNavigator's session\nhelper calls put_nonce(origin)
  Persist --> Held : new token returned on the final\nevent; the gate writes the one stream
}

state "401" as Denied

NoSession --> Mint : the client asks to establish a session,\noffering the GUID it holds

note left of Mint #fef3c7
  OPEN ARCHITECTURAL QUESTION — not resolved here.
  Where does session issuance live? Establishment is the
  one op that cannot present a session token, so it is
  either:
   (A) an unauthenticated op ON the Gate — keeps the single
       entrance, but weakens "no op is exempt from
       verification"; or
   (B) a SEPARATE endpoint — keeps every op authenticated,
       but weakens the single-entrance rule.
  No basis exists to prefer one. Minting confers nothing;
  resuming is a defect wherever it lives (R-6).
end note

MintGuid : a live, unexpired GUID is resumed
MintGuid : anything else begins a new session
MintGuid : (resume today is by knowledge of the GUID id;
MintGuid : security's view is it should require proof-of-
MintGuid : possession of the prior token — R-6, holds either way)
MintSign : token = "CH" + nonce(35) + guid(32)
MintSign : signature = RSA PKCS1v15 SHA256 over
MintSign : "origin:original_stamp:guid", signed with shared.key

Mint --> Live : SessionToken{origin, token, signature,\ncreated_at, signed, server_env}

Verify --> Denied : missing, malformed,\nor signature fails
Denied --> [*]

Live --> NoSession : expires_at passed,\nor the nonce is older than 300s

Verify : the client_sessioned call type — proof is the signed
Verify : session token; SessionToken.verify(expected_origin)
Verify : loads shared.crt, checks origin and length

Restamp : Nonce.restamp keeps the ORIGINAL stamp
Restamp : and replaces the LATEST one, so the token
Restamp : is signed afresh over an unchanged payload

Held : the GUID is stable for the whole session
Held : the nonce changes on every receive

note right of Denied #f3f4f6
  For every live-session op, none is exempt from
  verification, so a call cannot buy itself an answer by
  naming a harmless gesture. (Session ESTABLISHMENT is the
  one exception under debate — see the open question above.)
  Cookies are not used: HuggingFace's edge proxy strips
  credentials from OPTIONS preflights, so the token rides
  in the body the gate forwards.
end note

note bottom of Live #f3f4f6
  The UniversalNavigator is the only node that accepts
  external traffic; session verification, maintenance and
  timeouts are its helper functions, and the session is a
  repository row. Proxies carry tool-to-tool traffic only —
  no sessions.
end note

@enduml
